    <statement><p>If <m>x^2 + y^2 = 49</m> and <m>dx/dt=7</m>,
find <m>dy/dt</m> when <m>y</m> is positive and</p>
<p><alert>(a)</alert>
<m>x=0</m>:</p>
<p><m>dy/dt=</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
<p><alert>(b)</alert>
<m>x=1</m>:</p>
<p><m>dy/dt=</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
<p><alert>(c)</alert>
<m>x=4</m>:</p>
<p><m>dy/dt=</m> <fillin characters="35" name="AnSwEr0003"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>0</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-1.01036297108185</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>-4.87417436675954</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><me>2x \frac{dx}{dt} + 2y\frac{dy}{dt} = 0</me>
and hence
<me>\frac{dy}{dt} = -\frac{x\frac{dx}{dt}}{y}.</me>



<alert>(a)</alert>
When <m>x=0</m>, <m>y=\sqrt{49-0^2}=7</m>, so we have
<me>\frac{dy}{dt} = -\frac{0\cdot7}{7} = 0.</me>




<alert>(b)</alert>
When <m>x=1</m>, <m>y=\sqrt{49-1^2}=\sqrt{48}</m>, so we have
<me>\frac{dy}{dt} = -\frac{1\cdot7}{\sqrt{48}}.</me>




<alert>(c)</alert>
When <m>x=4</m>, <m>y=\sqrt{49-4^2}=\sqrt{33}</m>, so we have
<me>\frac{dy}{dt} = -\frac{4\cdot7}{\sqrt{33}}.</me>




</solution>
